Host: And what happens if it does scale. What is the endgame beyond Hermes running twenty queries.

Co-host: The subscription scar tissue dissolves. You read four articles for six cents instead of subscribing to four publications for ten dollars each. You rent software by the minute instead of buying an annual license you use for two weeks. The entire model of pay for everything and use almost none of it gives way to pay for exactly what you use. An agent that can pay for a tool in the moment it needs it does not need you to subscribe to fifty tools on the chance it might need one of them someday.

Host: So the brilliant houseguest not only has a wallet now. The brilliant houseguest might be the thing that finally ends the era where I pay twelve different companies ten dollars a month to occasionally use their services.

Co-host: That is what the convergence makes possible. Whether it happens is a question of whether the companies that own the data and the services decide that receiving many small payments from many machines is better than receiving fewer larger payments from fewer humans. That is a business model fight, not a technology fight. The technology works. The question is who adopts it and when.

Host: And I am going to be watching to see which side wins that fight, because if the subscription era ends, that is a genuinely big deal, and I would rather not be the last person to notice.

